While writing this test I had a question:  If both PSBTs have a `fallback_locktime` value, the "this" (receiving side) wins silently. Is this the expected behavior? Why not keep the max or directly fail merging if we have two conflicting fallback_locktimes? The test can be triggered with this mutant as one of the various examples:

```diff
diff --git a/src/psbt.cpp b/src/psbt.cpp
index 51fb19591a..29eb910014 100644
--- a/src/psbt.cpp
+++ b/src/psbt.cpp
@@ -56,7 +56,7 @@ bool PartiallySignedTransaction::Merge(const PartiallySignedTransaction& psbt)
     if (fallback_locktime == std::nullopt && psbt.fallback_locktime != std::nullopt) fallback_locktime = psbt.fallback_locktime;
 
     // Set m_tx_modifiable only if either PSBT had it set
-    if (m_tx_modifiable.has_value() || psbt.m_tx_modifiable.has_value()) {
+    if (m_tx_modifiable.has_value() && psbt.m_tx_modifiable.has_value()) {
         // In general, we AND the modifiable flags
         std::bitset<8> this_modifiable = m_tx_modifiable.value_or(0);
         std::bitset<8> psbt_modifiable = psbt.m_tx_modifiable.value_or(0);
```

Part of #36692. Please view this PR in context of changes like https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/36694.

`CTransaction` gains a constructor that allows direct declarative construction as an alternative to building through `CMutableTransaction`. `CTxIn`'s constructors gain an optional argument to pass the script witness.

It is a conscious decision that `vin/vout` are passed as generic input ranges and that only *their elements* are copied/moved.
Passing the `std::vector` by value and then moving the *container itself* into place would allow the following:

```cpp
std::vector<CTxIn> inputs = ...;
CTxIn& backdoor = inputs.back();

const CTransaction tx{std::move(inputs), ...};

backdoor = ...; // <- modify `tx`, potentially breaking its invariant
```

Adds a test to check that Knapsack skips overweight groups and continues checking the remaining groups.

**Mutant:** [Knapsack selection loop](https://bitcoincore.space/src/wallet/coinselection.cpp#3651)

```diff
if (group.m_weight > max_selection_weight) {
    max_weight_exceeded = true;
-   continue;
+   break;
}
```

I found this mutant on the mutation testing dashboard and confirmed it still survives on current master by applying it and running the five wallet test suites. All five passed with the mutant.

With this mutant, the loop stops at an overweight group instead of skipping it, potentially missing valid groups later in the list.
The test uses 10 overweight groups and one valid group. Since Knapsack shuffles groups randomly, it runs several times so that in some runs, overweight groups come before the valid one.
The test passes on master and fails with the mutant applied, producing `critical check result has failed`.
